# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — an AUTHOR GROUPING PAREN that spans a line break.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_author_paren_spans_rows`, exact text). A sweep would rewrite
# it into its own expected output and the cell would go green-vacuous.
#
# AXIS = the ANCHOR the row probe reads from. An element's recorded span ends at
# the WRAPPED NODE, so an author grouping `)` written after it lies OUTSIDE the
# span and INSIDE the region between this element and the next. A probe reading
# the bare `source[prev_end..cur_start]` therefore sees the newline the author
# put between `(1` and its own `)` and calls it a break BETWEEN elements — and
# the author's first row is silently split. `author_line_break_between` anchors
# on `advance_past_author_parens(prev_end)` for exactly this reason, which is
# the same reason its sibling `author_trailing_comma` does.
#
# ⚠ NOTHING ELSE IN THE TREE CAN SEE THIS CELL. It is corpus-neutral: 2898
# corpus files format identically with and without the anchor, so no output
# ratchet, no round-trip, no idempotence and no reparse gate moves. That is what
# makes this fixture — and the source pin
# `formatter_author_grouping_reads_one_site` — the only two things standing
# between the anchor and a future reader who deletes it as redundant.
#
# TWO cells, because the paren costume matters and the sibling family already
# measured that: TIGHT `(1\n)` and SPACED `( 3\n )`. Both must keep their row.
#
# RED-verified against the anchor-less build (Core #12): both rows split, giving
#     Vector[int] xs = [
#         (1),
#         2,
#         3, y,
#     ]
# — while every gate in the tree stayed green. Restored after measuring.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    int y = 4
    Vector[int] xs = [(1
), 2,
        3, y,
    ]
    Vector[int] ys = [( 5
 ), 6,
        7, y,
    ]
    print(xs.len() + ys.len())
